] [17592186420257 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420257 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $a,b,$ and $c$ be integers. If $a∣c,$ $b∣c,$ and $\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ then\n\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420257 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "237e8d15-d348-5681-a8d5-20db7f138f56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420257 #edb/ref 1074 "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we have that\n\n$$\na∣c,\\,b∣c,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1\\,⟹\\,ab∣c.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420258 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221381" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420258 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420258 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $11∣k$ then $33∣k$\n\nII. If $33∣k$ then $11∣k$\n\nIII. If $33∣k$ then $3∣k$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420258 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24815c66-50ab-5aab-ac7b-25ddc673e132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420258 #edb/ref 1074 "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we have transitivity of divisibility:\n\n$a∣b\\,$ and $\\,b∣c$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $a∣c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $k=11.$ Then, $11∣11$ but $33∤11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $11∣33.$ Therefore, we have\n$11∣33$ and $33∣k,$ which implies that $11∣k.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $3∣33.$ Therefore, we have\n$3∣33$ and $33∣k,$ which implies that $3∣k.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420259 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420259 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420259 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420259 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $x,y,$ and $z$ be integers. If $z∣xy$ and $\\text{gcd}(z,x)=1$ then\n\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420259 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "360410c4-4e76-5ee1-ae9a-a22c317dc4de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420259 #edb/ref 1074 "For any integers $x,$ $y,$ and $z,$ we have that\n\n$$\nz∣xy\\,\\text{and}\\,\\text{gcd}(z,x)=1\\,⟹\\,z∣y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420260 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420260 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420260 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420260 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $10\\,|\\,21b$ then $10\\,|\\,b$\n\nII. If $21\\,|\\,25b$ then $21\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If $15\\,|\\,9b$ then $15\\,|\\,b$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420260 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42a248b1-4b2a-5a7f-b673-589ac28da9a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420260 #edb/ref 1074 "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(10,21)=1.$ Therefore, we have $10\\,|\\,21b$ and $\\text{gcd}(10,21)=1,$ which implies $10\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(21,25)=1.$ Therefore, we have $21\\,|\\,25b$ and $\\text{gcd}(21,25)=1,$ which implies $21\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $b=5.$ Then, $15∣9\\cdot 5$ but $15∤5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420261 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420261 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420261 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If ${19}^{2}\\,|\\,19b$ then ${19}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n\nII. If $12\\,|\\,35b$ then $12\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If $22\\,|\\,{7}^{2}b$ then $22\\,|\\,b$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420261 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "553ed68f-8e74-5dda-a8b3-cdd3e89c31cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420261 #edb/ref 1074 "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $b=19.$ Then, ${19}^{2}∣19\\cdot 19$ but ${19}^{2}∤19.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(12,35)=1.$ Therefore, we have $12\\,|\\,35b$ and $\\text{gcd}(12,35)=1,$ which implies $12\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(22,{7}^{2})=1.$ Therefore, we have $22\\,|\\,{7}^{2}b$ and $\\text{gcd}(22,{7}^{2})=1,$ which implies $22\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420262 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420262 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420262 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420262 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $3\\,|\\,a$ and $2\\,|\\,a$ then $6\\,|\\,a$\n\nII. If $7\\,|\\,b$ and $14\\,|\\,b$ then $98\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If ${2}^{3}\\,|\\,c$ and $3\\,|\\,c$ then $24\\,|\\,c$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420262 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ddd359d-678b-53c8-9991-000fcf922944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420262 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$a\\,|\\,c,$ $\\,b\\,|\\,c,\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $ab\\,|\\,c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(2,3)=1$ and $2\\cdot 3=6.$ Therefore, we have $(2\\,|\\,a),(3\\,|\\,a),$ and $\\text{gcd}(2,3)=1,$ which implies $(6\\,|\\,a).$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $b=14.$ Then, $(7∣14)$ and $(14∣14),$ but $(98∤14).$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({2}^{3},3)=1$ and ${2}^